==English==

===Etymology 1===
Corruption of ''[[what]] [[cheer]]''.

"Wotcher" can also mean ''"What are you doing?"''
====Interjection====
{{en-intj}}

# {{context|mainly|British}} A [[friendly]] [[greeting]].
#:'''''Wotcher''', Harry! = How's it goin', Harry?''

"Wotcher" is most commonly used by the character, Nymphadora Tonks, in the last three books of the ''Harry Potter'' series when she appears.

===Etymology 2===
Abbreviation of ''[[what]] [[do]] [[you]]''.

====Pronunciation====
Generally pronounced 'wot-cha' but pronunciation depends on its place in a sentence.

By itself, it would be pronounced with the emphasis on the second syllable: "wot-CHA!"

In a sentence the second syllable would be suppressed and shortened e.g. "wotcher Harry" would be pronounced "wot-cha 'Arry".

As it is pronounced very quickly, it is sometimes mispronounced or misheard as "wod-ger".

===={{abbreviation}}====
'''wotcher'''

# {{context|mainly|British}} [[what]] [[do]] [[you]]
#:'''''Wotcher''' think?'' = What do you think?

